    "created_at": "2023-02-16T14:25:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-02-16T14:27:26Z",
    "body": "@Will-W 's comment reminded me of this so I came back :-) \r\n\r\nI just realized that it works _as desired_ for me with\r\n\r\n```\r\n[shlvl]\r\nthreshold = 1\r\nsymbol = \"^\"\r\nformat = \"[$symbol](red)\"\r\nrepeat = true\r\ndisabled = false\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut now that I'm reasoning through that it seems like I'm _depending_ on it being off by 1 -- I want one ^ if I'm in a subshell, which is to say SHLVL is 2. two ^^ if two subshells and SHLVL is 3, and so on.\r\n\r\nSo when I said it was fine in bash 5 and broken in bash 4, I don't think I was correct, even though I _like_ the behavior in bash 5! bash 4 had a different bug, involving thresholds.\r\n\r\nThe docs do say it _outputs_ SHLVL, though, and [the code and tests](https://github.com/starship/starship/blob/master/src/modules/shlvl.rs#L83) expect it to work as documented.\r\n\r\nHere's something I noticed, with defaults except for `threshold = 0`, in a brand new shell: \r\n\r\n```\r\n↕️  0 :) ~$ echo $SHLVL\r\n1\r\n↕️  0 :) ~$ echo $PS1\r\n\\[\\]↕️ \\[\\]\\[\\]0\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]:)\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]~\\[\\]\\$\r\n↕️  0 :) ~$ starship prompt\r\n\\[\\]↕️  \\[\\]\\[\\]1\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]:)\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]~\\[\\]\\$\r\n```\r\n\r\nas you can see the prompt has SHLVL of 0, but the output of `starship prompt` has SHLVL of 1. So that's a pointer to \"SHLVL is not what we expect at the time we evaluate it\". \r\n\r\n(And separately, once this is fixed, I think the shlvl plugin could benefit from an option to subtract 1 from the output, so that you can use this as \"number of subshells\". **Edit**: Which is https://github.com/starship/starship/issues/4815 linked above!)",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
