    "updated_at": "2023-02-16T14:44:44Z",
    "body": "A little progress! The unexpected behavior is triggered by `$( )` (which is how starship sets PS1 in `starship_precmd`. Check this out:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ /usr/local/bin/starship prompt --terminal-width=\"$COLUMNS\" --status=$STARSHIP_CMD_STATUS --pipestatus=\"${STARSHIP_PIPE_STATUS[*]}\" --jobs=\"$NUM_JOBS\"\r\n\\[\\]↕️  \\[\\]\\[\\]1\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]:)\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]~\\[\\]\\$\r\n``` \r\nand yet,\r\n```\r\n$ OUT=$(/usr/local/bin/starship prompt --terminal-width=\"$COLUMNS\" --status=$STARSHIP_CMD_STATUS --pipestatus=\"${STARSHIP_PIPE_STATUS[*]}\" --jobs=\"$NUM_JOBS\")\r\n$ echo $OUT\r\n\\[\\]↕️ \\[\\]\\[\\]0\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]:)\\[\\]\\[\\] \\[\\]\\[\\]~\\[\\]\\$\r\n```\r\n\r\nI can't reproduce this with pure bash, presumably it's optimizing itself to not have to fork:\r\n\r\n```\r\n$ a() { echo $SHLVL; }\r\n$ a\r\n1\r\n$ echo $(a)\r\n1\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut as long as I fork in `$()` then it's reproducible: \r\n\r\n```\r\n$ f() { perl -e 'print $ENV{\"SHLVL\"}'; }\r\n$ f\r\n1\r\n$ echo $(f)\r\n0\r\n```\r\n\r\nIs this a bash bug, I wonder?",
