    "updated_at": "2023-07-12T15:06:55Z",
    "body": "Just dropping another use case to show this:\r\n1. Use a default non-`bash` prompt\r\n2. Configure starship both in `bash` and your non-`bash` prompt\r\n3. Open a terminal\r\n4. Run `bash`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nIn my use case I have `zsh` as the default (on a Linux system), but I sometimes need to switch to `bash` for specific needs.  I'd like my prompt to match the `SHLVL` variable, but it doesn't.  The prompt in `bash` always indicate 1 less than the current `SHLVL` value.\r\n\r\nHowever if I run `zsh` again, to get a `zsh`-in-`zsh` nested shell, it does indicate the nesting level matching the `SHLVL` correctly.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nThe easiest case to test any of our conditions is to just run `bash` again.  If you're already on an interactive shell prompt, that will give you a nested shell prompt using bash:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# opens terminal window\r\n$ echo $SHLVL\r\n1\r\n$ bash\r\n$ echo $SHLVL\r\n2\r\n$ exit\r\n$ echo $SHLVL\r\n1\r\n```\r\n\r\nThough do note that it's possible to configure your terminal window to open a shell that's already nested if you didn't change your system-wide default shell for your user, so it may report `2` and `3` instead of `1` and `2` respectively.",
