    "created_at": "2024-12-19T16:53:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-12-19T16:57:03Z",
    "body": "It's almost 2025 and I'm amazed this is still an open issue. Starship's $shlvl is zero-based ( which is why a threshold of 1 refers to the first subshell and not the initial shell) and bash's $SHLVL is one-based (Manpage: \"SHLVL  Incremented by one each time an instance of bash is started.\").\r\n\r\nThere is a simpler fix for this: keep $shlvl as zero-based and add $SHLVL for one-based output.\r\n\r\nIn the meantime, a livable workaround is to have shlvl indicate the number of sublevels:\r\n```\r\n[shlvl]\r\n  # the builtin $shlvl is zero-based versus SHLVL is one-based\r\n  # so use $shlvl to indicate number of sublevels\r\n  disabled = false\r\n  threshold = 1\r\n  format = '[\\(+]($style)[$shlvl]($style)[\\) ]($style)'\r\n  style = \"fg:#666666\"\r\n```\r\nResults in `prompt$ ` for the initial level and `(+1) prompt$ ` for the first subshell.",
